---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The U.S. Navy method estimates body fat from a few tape-measure readings rather than calipers or
      a scan. Men enter their neck and waist; women add their hips. Combined with your height, those
      circumferences go into a formula that returns a body fat percentage. Add your bodyweight and the
      calculator also splits it into fat mass and lean mass. A man of 175 cm with a 38 cm neck and
      85 cm waist estimates at about 17 percent body fat, which is the fitness range on the standard
      scale.
      Also a body fat percentage calculator and a body composition calculator, this navy body fat calculator
      reports your body composition and lean body mass from a simple circumference measurement.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Body Fat Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How the Navy Method Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Fat and muscle sit differently around the body. Fat tends to gather at the waist and hips, while
      the neck stays relatively lean, so the gap between those measurements carries information about
      overall composition. The Navy formula was fitted against underwater weighing, the gold-standard
      lab method, so that a handful of tape readings can approximate a result that normally needs a
      tank of water. It needs no equipment beyond a cloth tape, which is why it became a practical
      field test in the first place.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="where-to-measure">
    <h2>Where to Measure <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Accuracy lives and dies on how you take neck, waist, and hip measurements correctly.
      Take the neck just below the larynx, letting
      the tape slope slightly downward at the front. Men measure the waist horizontally at the navel;
      women take the waist at its narrowest point and the hips at the widest. Keep the tape level and
      snug against the skin without digging in, and breathe normally rather than holding your breath or
      pulling your stomach in. Measure in the morning before eating, and use the same routine every
      time so your readings can be compared.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="fat-and-lean-mass">
    <h2>Fat Mass and Lean Mass <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Body fat percentage on its own is useful, but the split it implies is often more motivating.
      Enter your bodyweight to find fat mass and lean mass from bodyweight: the tool multiplies it by
      the percentage to give your fat mass, then
      subtracts that to give your lean mass, which is everything else: muscle, bone, organs, and water.
      When you are training, watching lean mass hold steady while fat mass falls is a much better sign
      than the scale alone, because the scale cannot tell the two apart.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Track the trend, not the number">
      <p>Any single tape reading carries a few points of error. Measure the same way each week and follow the direction of travel over a month. A falling trend is real progress even if one reading blips up.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="body-fat-vs-bmi">
    <h2>Body Fat vs BMI <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      BMI uses only height and weight, so it treats a kilogram of muscle exactly like a kilogram of
      fat. That is why a lean, muscular person can be flagged as overweight by BMI while carrying very
      little fat. Body fat percentage measures composition directly, so it answers the question BMI
      cannot: how much of your weight is actually fat. Comparing body fat percentage versus BMI, the two
      work well together, with BMI as the fast first screen and body fat as the closer look.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p><strong>Man, 175 cm, 38 cm neck, 85 cm waist.</strong> For example, the Navy formula returns about 17 percent body fat, the fitness range on the standard scale.</p>
    <p><strong>Woman, 165 cm, 32 cm neck, 74 cm waist, 96 cm hips.</strong> For example, that estimates near 24 percent, and at 62 kg it splits into roughly 15 kg fat mass and 47 kg lean mass.</p>
    <p><strong>Same man, tape pulled tight.</strong> For example, two readings differ when the tape is pulled tighter: a 2 cm smaller waist can drop the estimate two whole points, which is why a snug, level tape matters.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Pulling the tape too tight">
      <p>
        A tape that compresses the skin reads smaller and skews the estimate low. Snug but not
        digging in is the rule. If the tape leaves a mark, it was too tight.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Measuring inconsistently">
      <p>
        Different times of day, after meals, or at slightly different spots each week make readings
        impossible to compare. Fix a routine, measure first thing in the morning, and repeat it
        exactly so the trend means something.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="An estimate, not a scan">
      <p>The tape method is a practical field estimate with a few points of error, not a lab measurement. For a precise figure, a DEXA scan is the reference. Everything here runs in your browser and never leaves your device.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
